```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: switch1
diagnosis_text:
switch1's startup configuration incorrectly assigns laptop0's access port
(eth7 / fa7) to the VLAN20 bridge (br_vlan20), while the adjacent user
laptops — laptop2 on eth3 and laptop3 on eth4 — are both in the VLAN10
bridge (br_vlan10). As a result, laptop0 gets its address from the
172.16.20.0/24 subnet (172.16.20.10, gateway 172.16.20.1), while laptop2
and laptop3 are on 172.16.10.0/24. The two groups are in completely separate
broadcast domains. Local file-sharing and service-discovery traffic (SMB
NetBIOS, mDNS, etc.) is broadcast-scoped and never crosses the VLAN
boundary, so neither laptop can discover or reach the other at the
application layer — consistent with the user's report of "nothing in either
direction." ICMP technically still flows through router0's inter-VLAN
routing (ttl=63), but that is invisible to the file-sharing stack.

The lab was started this morning (switch1.startup mtime 2026-08-07 04:51
UTC), meaning the misconfiguration was baked in from the first boot. VLAN20
contains only one end-host (laptop0), which is a structural anomaly
confirming it was meant to hold additional devices or that laptop0 was meant
to be in VLAN10 alongside laptop2 and laptop3.

Root cause: switch1.startup assigns eth7 to br_vlan20 instead of
br_vlan10, isolating laptop0 in a separate subnet from its office neighbors.

Corrective fix: On switch1, detach eth7 from br_vlan20 and attach it to
br_vlan10 (`ip link set eth7 nomaster; ip link set eth7 master br_vlan10`),
then reconfigure laptop0's IP address to a 172.16.10.x address with gateway
172.16.10.1, and update switch1.startup to make the fix persistent.
=== END ===
